From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000006 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
Date: Mon, 25 Aug 2025 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ingenic-sdk: t31 motor - stop TCU counter when motors idle

The Pan/Tilt stepper driver free-runs its TCU channel after the boot-time
homing/positioning moves complete. motor_ops_move() enables the counter
(TESR) to drive the stepper; once the move reaches its target the
interrupt handler sets both motors to MOTOR_OPS_STOP but never disables
the channel, so the counter keeps counting at MOTOR_DEF_SPEED (900) and
raising the full-match interrupt every period forever - even while the
camera is sitting still.

On a 24 MHz ext clock with /64 prescale that is 24e6/64/900 = 900
interrupts/sec of pure dead work on IRQ 202 (jz_timer_interrupt). The
idle handler still calls motor_move_step() and writes the phase GPIOs on
every tick, and it keeps the single-core T31 constantly preempted, which
surfaces as a general "sluggish" feel on motorized cameras.

Disable the counter in the idle branch once both motors have reached
MOTOR_OPS_STOP and the final step phase is driven out. A subsequent move
re-enables it via motor_ops_move() -> jz_tcu_enable_counter() (TESR), so
motion is unaffected. Confirmed on a Cinnado D1 T31L: idle drops from
~900/sec to 0/sec, and a live move still steps normally.

Signed-off-by: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
---
--- a/3.10.14/misc/motor/motor.c
+++ b/3.10.14/misc/motor/motor.c
@@ -419,6 +419,12 @@ static irqreturn_t jz_timer_interrupt(int irq, void *dev_id)
 			complete(&mdev->stop_completion);
 		}
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_SOC_T40
+		ingenic_tcu_counter_stop(mdev->tcu);
+#else
+		jz_tcu_disable_counter(mdev->tcu);
+#endif
+
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
